                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: United Kingdom\nTime Period: Early 1900s (name pontoon from WWI); vingt-un from late 18th century in France and Britain\nHistorical Development: British domestic version of Twenty-one; name from French vingt-et-un; first rules in Britain 1800\nCultural Significance: Popular family game in Britain; 3rd most popular card game in Britain (1981) after Rummy and Whist',
